But yeah, I think we should rewrite the code to make it a bit easier to understand. Potentially something like:
```c++
STAmount x{};
x = v1.iou() + v2.iou();
```

or we could make `STAmount` support constructing from an `IOUAmount`

You might want to restore the original behavior:

```
auto const value = val.value();
mIsNegative = value < 0;
mValue = mIsNegative ? -value : value;
```

so the `.value()` is only called once instead of twice.

The original code has:

```
if (native())
{
    set(XRPAmount{num});
}
else if (mAsset.holds<MPTIssue>())
{
    set(MPTAmount{num});
}
else
{
    Throw<std::runtime_error>("Unknown integral asset type");
}
mOffset = 0;
```

but this has now seemingly changed to:
```
if (native())
{
    set(XRPAmount{num});
}
else 
{
    set(MPTAmount{num});
}
mOffset = 0;
```

Is that intentional?

Doesn't this constructor also call normalize?  So we've done the work twice?

```suggestion
    mantissa_ = v.mantissa();
    exponent_ = v.exponent();
```

Yes, that's correct. `IOUAmount(v)` constructor has these two checks alredy.
```cpp
 if (exponent_ > kMaxExponent)
     Throw<std::overflow_error>("value overflow");
if (exponent_ < kMinExponent)
    *this = beast::kZero;
```

No change needed, but I think this would be more readable as standalone `if` statements:

```
    auto mode = Number::getround();

    if (mode == towards_zero)
        return -1;

    if (mode == downward)
    {
        if (sbit_)
        {
            if (digits_ > 0 || xbit_)
                return 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    if (mode == upward)
    {
        if (sbit_)
            return -1;
        if (digits_ > 0 || xbit_)
            return 1;
        return -1;
    }

    // assume round to nearest if mode is not one of the predefined values
    if (digits_ > 0x5000'0000'0000'0000)
        return 1;
    if (digits_ < 0x5000'0000'0000'0000)
        return -1;
    if (xbit_)
        return 1;
    return 0;
```

The reason I like the -1/1 int is because of this line:
```c++
    mantissa_ = xm * xn;
```
Yes, I could rewrite it with:
```c++
    if (xn)
        mantissa_ = -xm;
    else
        mantissa_ = xm;
```
but I really like the conciseness, and potential branch elimination of this formulation.

For this definition of `isnormal()` the only de-normalized number that is allowed is 0, which should be taken care of by the checks above this assert.  If this assert fires, there is a serious bug in `Number` and should be investigated immediately.

One way for de-normalized numbers to arise is via the "unchecked" constructor.  Clients that use this constructor should take care to only construct normalized numbers.  This constructor is an optimization for creating constants, while avoiding the normalization process which the coder can do manually, for example:
```c++
    constexpr Number a2{1405502114116773, -17, unchecked{}};
    static_assert(a2.isnormal());
```

Do we expect the user to normalize the arguments before calling this operator? Would the following code work?
```
Number a{1,0};
Number b{1,-1}, c{9,-1};
assert(a == b+c);
```

